/*
Problem: Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST (LC 235)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Use the Binary Search Tree property.

• If both nodes are smaller than the
  current node, the LCA lies in the
  left subtree.

• If both nodes are greater than the
  current node, the LCA lies in the
  right subtree.

• Otherwise, the current node is the
  point where the paths split, so it
  is the Lowest Common Ancestor.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. If the tree is empty,
   return NULL.

2. If both p and q are smaller than
   the current node, recurse left.

3. If both p and q are greater than
   the current node, recurse right.

4. Otherwise, return the current
   node as the LCA.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

          6
         / \
        2   8
       / \ / \
      0  4 7  9
        / \
       3   5

p = 2

q = 8

Answer:

6

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

          6
         / \
        2   8
       / \
      0   4
         / \
        3   5

p = 2

q = 4

Answer:

2

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The BST property lets us determine
which subtree contains both nodes.

The first node where the two paths
diverge (or one node equals the
current node) is the Lowest Common
Ancestor.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(h)

h = Height of the BST.

Balanced BST: O(log n)

Worst case (skewed): O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

Space : O(h)

Recursion stack.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Search Tree / Recursion
*/

class Solution {
public:

    TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(
        TreeNode* root,
        TreeNode* p,
        TreeNode* q) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return NULL;

        // Both nodes are in the left subtree
        if (root->val > p->val &&
            root->val > q->val) {

            return lowestCommonAncestor(
                root->left, p, q);
        }

        // Both nodes are in the right subtree
        if (root->val < p->val &&
            root->val < q->val) {

            return lowestCommonAncestor(
                root->right, p, q);
        }

        // Split point (or one node is root)
        return root;
    }
};
